From: Fathom <fathom@localhost>
Subject: [PATCH] usb: dwc2: set the PHY mode for peripheral-only and OTG ports

dwc2 initialises and powers on its generic PHY but never tells it which
role the port has. Some PHYs only assert VBUS valid when told they are in
device mode: the Ingenic X1000 PHY sets VBUSVLDEXT/VBUSVLDEXTSEL and
OTG_DISABLE in its set_mode callback. On boards where VBUS is not wired to
the SoC (the HiFi Walker H2 senses the cable through its PMIC), the
controller therefore never sees a session and never connects to the host.

After powering the PHY on, call phy_set_mode(PHY_MODE_USB_DEVICE) for a
peripheral-only port (dr_mode = "peripheral") and PHY_MODE_USB_OTG for an
OTG port, where the X1000 PHY also enables OTG and follows the ID pin. PHYs
without a set_mode callback are unaffected.

Signed-off-by: Fathom
---
diff -Naur a/drivers/usb/dwc2/platform.c b/drivers/usb/dwc2/platform.c
--- a/drivers/usb/dwc2/platform.c
+++ b/drivers/usb/dwc2/platform.c
@@ -124,6 +124,23 @@
 			if (ret)
 				phy_exit(hsotg->phy);
 		}
+		/*
+		 * A port may have no VBUS sense line to the PHY (the board
+		 * detects the cable elsewhere, e.g. in a PMIC). Tell the PHY
+		 * the port's mode so it can assert VBUS valid itself and, for
+		 * an OTG port, follow the ID pin.
+		 */
+		if (ret == 0 && hsotg->phy) {
+			enum usb_dr_mode dr = usb_get_dr_mode(hsotg->dev);
+			int err = 0;
+
+			if (dr == USB_DR_MODE_PERIPHERAL)
+				err = phy_set_mode(hsotg->phy, PHY_MODE_USB_DEVICE);
+			else if (dr == USB_DR_MODE_OTG)
+				err = phy_set_mode(hsotg->phy, PHY_MODE_USB_OTG);
+			if (err && err != -EOPNOTSUPP)
+				dev_warn(hsotg->dev, "failed to set PHY mode: %d\n", err);
+		}
 	}
 
 	if (ret)
